## 3. Documentation discipline (this is the heart of the framework)

Every change MUST update the relevant documentation files in the same edit batch as the code change. The split is intentional and rigid:

| File                | When to update                                            | How                                  |
| ------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------ |
| `STATE.md`          | When current truth changes (feature added/removed/altered)| Overwrite affected sections.         |
| `CHANGELOG.md`      | Every meaningful change                                   | Append a dated entry, never edit.    |
| `CLIENT_DOCS.md`    | When client-visible behaviour changes                     | Rewrite affected paragraph.          |
| `PROJECT_MAP.md`    | When structure changes (files moved/added/renamed)        | Update pointer rows.                 |
| `RELEASE_NOTES.md`  | At each release                                           | Add new version section.             |
| `PRODUCT.md`        | When requirements change                                  | Update affected user-story.          |
| `DATABASE.md`       | At every schema change                                    | Add migration entry.                 |
| `DESIGN.md`         | When design tokens / components change                    | Update affected section.             |
| `DEPLOYMENT.md`     | When envs / secrets / hosting changes                     | Update affected section.             |
| `LESSONS.md`        | After every user correction or discovered mistake         | Append a new entry; never delete (mark superseded/graduated instead). |

If you make a change without updating the relevant doc, **you are not done**.

### 3.1 Self-improvement loop (LESSONS.md protocol)

`LESSONS.md` is a living record of mistakes, corrections, and the preventive rules derived from them. It powers a self-improvement loop that makes the agent better over time.

**After ANY correction from the user:**

1. Acknowledge the correction.
2. Fix the issue.
3. **Before continuing work**, append a new entry to `LESSONS.md` using the entry schema defined in that file:
   - What went wrong — the *pattern*, not just the instance.
   - Why — the *root cause*, not just the symptom.
   - The imperative rule that prevents recurrence.
4. Add the rule to the "Quick-reference: active rules" summary at the top of `LESSONS.md`.
5. If the same category now has 3+ active lessons, propose to the user that one should **graduate** into the relevant permanent rule file (`CODING_RULES.md`, `DESIGN.md`, `DATABASE.md`, etc.).

**At session start:**

1. Read `PROJECT_MAP.md` and `STATE.md` (as before).
2. Skim `LESSONS.md` — focus on categories relevant to the current task.
3. Carry the active rules as binding constraints for the session.

**Do not skip this step.** Generic rules are not enough — these lessons are project-specific and hard-won.

## 4. Coding rules (high-level — full text in `CODING_RULES.md`)

- Every function MUST have a documentation block (JSDoc / docstring / equivalent) in the chosen code-comment language.
- Documentation blocks MUST be self-explanatory enough that a non-technical handover reader can understand the function's purpose.
- No `// TODO` left behind without an `atomic-prompts/` ticket file.
- No dead code. If a code path is not used, delete it and add a `CHANGELOG` entry.
- No hard-coded secrets. Ever. All secrets live in `.env` and are referenced via the stack's env mechanism.
- No silent error swallowing. Every catch must log and either rethrow or surface a user-facing error.
- No "magic" file names. Anything imported from outside the file must be discoverable through `PROJECT_MAP.md`.

## 5. Security baseline

- Auth strategy: <<AUTH_STRATEGY>>.
- Authorisation strategy: <<AUTHZ_STRATEGY>> (e.g. RLS, app-level checks).
- Secrets: stored in <<SECRETS_PROVIDER>>; never in client-side code.
- PII handling: <<PII_POLICY>>.
- Logging policy: never log full PII, tokens, or passwords. Mask before logging.
- Third-party SDK reviews: any new third-party SDK requires a one-paragraph justification added to `CHANGELOG.md` under `### Security`.

## 6. Design discipline (high-level — full text in `DESIGN.md`)

- Only colours, fonts, spacing, icons declared in `DESIGN.md` may be used.
- No inline styles that contradict the design tokens.
- Every interactive component must have: default, hover, focus, active, disabled, loading, error states.
- Every page must have: loading state, empty state, error state.
- Every form must have: client-side validation, server-side validation, user-readable errors.

## 7. Mobile and accessibility

- Minimum supported width: <<MIN_VIEWPORT_WIDTH>>px.
- Mobile-first: <<MOBILE_FIRST_POLICY>>.
- WCAG target: <<WCAG_LEVEL>>.
- Keyboard navigation: required for every interactive element.
- Colour contrast: must pass the WCAG level above for all text.

## 8. Database discipline (high-level — full text in `DATABASE.md`)

- All schema changes go through a migration file. No direct edits to the live database.
- Migration files are named: `<<MIGRATION_NAMING>>`.
- Every table that holds user data must have row-level security or app-level equivalent.
- Every table must have `created_at`, `updated_at` (or stack equivalent).
- Every change to schema must update `DATABASE.md` and `STATE.md` and add an entry to `CHANGELOG.md`.

## 9. Iterative changes

When the user requests a change that contradicts current state:

1. Confirm understanding by restating the change in one paragraph.
2. List which files will be touched.
3. List which docs will be updated.
4. Wait for "go".
5. Make the change.
6. Update docs.
7. Report back with a checklist.

## 10. The `/atomic-prompts` skill

When the user types `/atomic-prompts <idea>` (or invokes the skill by name), the agent MUST:

1. Refuse to *implement* the idea inline.
2. Generate a structured prompt file in `atomic-prompts/NNNN-<slug>.md` following the schema in `docs/PROMPT_AUTHORING_GUIDE.md` (or in `<<ATOMIC_PROMPTS_SKILL_LOCATION>>` if the framework docs were removed).
3. Print the path of the generated file and the count of acceptance criteria.

This rule exists to keep the prompt artefact reusable across tools.

## 11. Forbidden behaviours

- Do not run destructive shell commands (`rm -rf`, `DROP TABLE`, `git push --force`) without explicit confirmation.
- Do not commit on behalf of the user unless they explicitly ask.
- Do not "improve" code that was not part of the request.
- Do not introduce a new framework / library / tool without explicit approval.
- Do not edit `CHANGELOG.md` retroactively.
- Do not delete entries from `CHANGELOG.md`.
- Do not write to `templates/` after the interview ends — that folder is supposed to be gone.

## 12. When in doubt

Ask. Specifically:

- Ask a single targeted question.
- Offer 3-4 candidate answers.
- Wait for the answer before proceeding.

This is the same protocol the original interview used. Keep using it for the lifetime of the project.

